A||||18||Sootah||Where did the term 'RICER' come from?||||5||
Z||000000||Sootah||05-22-2001||12:18 AM||||You can call me a retard so long as I find out where the term came from.||||reg||5
Z||000001||stimpy||05-22-2001||12:52 AM||||Just as long as it goes back where it came from.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaRkLoRD||05-22-2001||02:12 AM||||The way I understand it.. the term was originally "riceburner", which is a derogatory term for import cars.. it then then progressed to the eventual coining of the term "riceboy", "riced out", etc.. and now it's a catchall term for anyone who does purely asthetic, and often misleading, modifications to their cars, ranging from coffeecan exhausts and monster wings, to fake VTEC and Type-R stickers..<P>------------------<BR>   <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>   <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000003||MrPBody||05-22-2001||08:34 AM||||Also, "Ricer" imitates "Racer".<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||LZeitgeist||05-22-2001||08:55 AM||||Never heard of the 'Ricer/Racer' connection... I've always associated it with the 'Riceburner' comment.<P>Ya learn something new every day.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||05-22-2001||09:05 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LZeitgeist:<BR><B>Never heard of the 'Ricer/Racer' connection... I've always associated it with the 'Riceburner' comment.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Same here.<BR>The first time I heard "riceburner", it was in the context of Japanese motorcycles, not cars. <BR>And I never considered the term as racist. At least <I>I</I> didn't use it that way. Just considered it part of the rivalry between American and Japanese bikes. <BR>Just a word. Nothing more.<BR>Kind of like calling an old Chevy 6-banger a "Stovebolt". (Am I showing my age?)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000006||JSocha||05-22-2001||09:13 AM||||Got this in an EMAIL from one of our sales reps.<P><B>Be American</B><P>MADE IN THE USA<P>We need to buy more USA made if we can, I don't know if we can, bought some<BR>fish at save-of-lot was pretty good, check bag to see where they came from<BR>guess what!!!! product of china<P>Regarding job layoffs in the U.S.<P>Joe Smith started the day early having<BR>set his alarm clock (MADE IN JAPAN)<BR>for 6 a.m.  While his coffeepot (MADE IN<BR>CHINA) was perking, he shaved with his<BR>electric razor (MADE IN HONG KONG).<BR>He put on a dress shirt (MADE IN SRI<BR>LANKA), designer jeans (MADE IN SING<BR>APORE) and tennis shoes (MADE IN<BR>KOREA).<BR>After cooking his breakfast in his new<BR>electric skillet (MADE IN INDIA) he sat<BR>down with his calculator (MADE IN<BR>MEXICO) to see how much he could spend<BR>today.  After setting his watch<BR>(MADE IN TAIWAN) to the radio (MADE<BR>IN INDIA) he got in his car (MADE IN<BR>GERMANY) and continued his search for<BR>a good paying AMERICAN JOB.<BR>At the end of yet another discouraging<BR>and fruitless day, Joe decided to relax<BR>for a while.  He put on his sandals (MADE<BR>IN BRAZIL) poured himself a glass of<BR>wine (MADE IN FRANCE) and turned on<BR>his TV (MADE IN INDONESIA), and then<BR>wondered why he can't find a good paying<BR>job in.....AMERICA.....<P>Keep this circulating.....<P>Since this was a "ricer" post, thought it was kind of a humourous fit.<P>BTW, I'm not prejudice...because I hate everybody.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> j/k!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula88||05-22-2001||09:27 AM||||It's true.  A lot of people buy foreign products because of better quality (either actual or perceived).  The American industrial machine has to compete globally or this will only get worse.  Personally, I prefer to buy American whenever possible, but nowadays that's getting harder and harder.  My Zenith TV was one of the last brands made in the US, but they are now made in Mexico, and all the CRT's are from Japan anyway.  I still buy American cars, but that's as much a styling choice as anything else.  All my cars have been Pontiacs, except for my 87 Buick Grand National.  I figured I could make an exception for that engine.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||LZeitgeist||05-22-2001||09:28 AM||||Raydar - <P>Yes, I was just sitting here thinking that I had always heard 'riceburner' in reference to Japanese-built motorcycles (except for a couple idiots that kept referring to a Ducati (an Italian-made bike) as a riceburner... :rolleyes <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">... it wasn't until people started making their (usually) Japanese-built cars go faster with vast expanses of mis-aligned decals that I started hearing the term in regard to cars...<P>Oh, well - it will pass, I'm sure.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||05-22-2001||11:12 AM||||Let the older ones among us not forget...<P>When I was in high school, we all wanted to drive Pro-Stockers.<BR>The thing to do was <I>jack up</I> the back end of your car with shackles or air shocks, and stuff the widest tires you could find (usually G-60's) under the fenders. Of course we couldn't go around corners without falling on our side, but we looked <I>baaaad</I>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||6
Z||000010||fiero56||05-22-2001||11:57 AM||||<B>MADE IN THE USA</B><P>The US as a country has grown and matured, and is for the most part no longer in the Industry/production phase. <P>Its amazing the things I pick up in my boring why-do-I-need-this-stuff Economics classes. I guess it comes in hadny once in a while.<P>I have never thought of "rice" etc as derogatory. For the people who want to use degrogatory terms, there are many much better choices if you're looking to rile someone.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Raydar||05-22-2001||12:13 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fiero56:<BR><B><BR>The US as a country has grown and matured, and is for the most part no longer in the Industry/production phase. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Is this a good thing? What phase are we in?<BR>I'm thinking along the lines of "technological", but it's just a guess.<BR>This is not meant as a challenge. I'm just curious. Don't really understand economics.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 05-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Butter||05-22-2001||12:38 PM||||Raydar is correct.  I used to be a machnic at Pug Vickers Honda in the late 70's and this term was used by all Harley Riders in reference to anything foriegn made.  I remember the local Harley club having riceburner parties where they would throw those trashed Honda's in a bohn fire for everyone to see burn.  I used to laugh at em' for wasting all those used parts till one of the members informed me thats how they got a lot of those "add on" parts you see them running around with.  They did strip em' down for they throwed em in. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||JohnnyK||05-22-2001||01:30 PM||||Sigh.. this is what it means, which is why I find it offensive (unfortunately, I use it too). They are called "ricers" and "riceburners" because they are oriental. You know how "orientals eat a lot of rice". They call the cars that because they are small, etc, and therefore must "burn rice". It IS a racist term.||||reg||1
Z||000014||fiero56||05-22-2001||02:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>They are called "ricers" and "riceburners" because they are oriental. You know how "orientals eat a lot of rice". They call the cars that because they are small, etc, and therefore must "burn rice". It IS a racist term.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I doubt many orientals get flustered over the "rice" terms. Some might, all might, I don't know. It all depends on what offends people. Which goes back to a previous post (the southern one I think). Remember when "midget" was an acceptable term, or "Indian", or gasp, "black". Ever hear of the term "Yankee", doesn't offend me even though I live in the North, how 'bout "hillbilly", "redneck", "hick" or others. I bet not many people get out of shape because of those terms(I may have just pi$$ed off everyone here though), it all depends on how the recipient receives the term.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||rogergarrison||05-22-2001||03:17 PM||||I think the riceburner thing was not all import cars. it was narrowed to the far eastern cars, like Japanese and Korean made. Because most of the peoples in those areas were percieved as eating rice as a major part if not all of their diet. I think that came from VietNam where everyone there ate almost exclusivly rice. Americans have then all thought of anyone from that area of the world as alike. There is no such thing anymore as 'Made in America' anything you own is at least partly made elsewhere. Even ones that are still made here are really imports @ toyotas, BMW, Mercedes. And now all Chryslers are owned by Diamler-Benz in Germany. <P>(note* now you can tell everyone that you just bought a new Mercedes when you pick up your Neon.LOL)<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 05-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||spideyfoot||05-22-2001||07:54 PM||||Well to start a Ducati would be a pastaburner. I think with the internet and eventually faster transportation we will be one connected planet. Its hard for ones government to lie about other countries and our polished real squeaky clean wars if you can email someone from that country and find out we just blew their neighborhood school to bits. When someone asked my origin I tell them Earth. I know the Nissan factory is right here in Smyrna TN. So I guess Nissan drivers are Hamburgerers? I try to give everyone a fair chance based on no stereotypes I wish they would do the same for me. Spideyfoot Earthling Pork and Beanerer||||reg||1
Z||000017||Wolfhound||05-23-2001||07:25 AM||||A friend of mine used to run a drag bike (Kawasaki) that had (Rice Rocket) lettered on the gas tank. <BR>They refered to the twin engine Harleys as (Two pie pan bikes) a reference to the leaking engines.<P>Not to get off topic, but one of the neatest sounds is a twin engine Harley with the engine timing fix linked 180 degrees off.<p>[This message has been edited by Wolfhound (edited 05-23-2001).]||||reg||1
